"element is not stable" and raf log shows only one frame at 162, then no frames for ~2s! So after the game loop's single frame, there are no more RAF frames. Playwright's stability check requires 2 frames, and frames aren't produced because... nothing invalidates and headless doesn't schedule. When we earlier measured menu fps 45, our measurement's own rAF callbacks forced frames to be scheduled continuously. But Playwright's internal rAF (injected script) maybe also schedules frames? It does, but perhaps its rAF is throttled because the page isn't visible? Hmm.

But eventually after 6s it gets frames. Why? Possibly the mouse movement to the element triggers hover invalidation causing frames. Playwright stability check likely occurs before moving mouse; it may be stuck waiting for frames that never come because page is idle. When some external event triggers a frame, it advances. The 6s maybe because our repeated trial attempts? Each trial click attempt might move mouse? Not sure.

Simplest robust fix: keep a lightweight animation running on the menu so frames are produced. Our earlier idle test "menu fps with blank raf" didn't help because measurement itself scheduled frames; but maybe a blank rAF loop keeps compositor frames? Let's test: add a persistent blank rAF loop, wait 500ms, then trial click. Previously in idle.cjs we added blank rAF, then measured fps (which itself schedules), then trial ok in 1248ms. Hmm still 1.2s but succeeded. But that was before CSS fix. Let's test directly with a page that has continuous cheap RAF loop vs not.

Maybe keep the menu render loop running continuously but at 30 fps, which keeps frames and looks alive (clouds/sun moving). But render costs 500ms on software → blocks and makes things worse. Instead run a cheap update that invalidates something small (e.g., animate a CSS property or draw a tiny canvas). We need frame production. A persistent rAF loop that reschedules itself should cause the compositor to produce frames if it has work; but a no-op might not. We can force repaint via a tiny DOM change: toggle a hidden 1x1 element opacity or transform. That would produce frames but lightweight. Better: animate the start-glow's CSS transform via a CSS animation — that's a compositor animation and produces frames continuously, cheaply (transform only, though filter blur on it may be expensive). But earlier with glowPulse + backdrop etc. it was heavy. Without backdrop and balance, maybe transform animation is cheap. We could animate `.start-glow` with transform/opacity (compositor) to keep frames; at 1440x900 the glow layer itself is large (~900px) with blur filter; transform animation forces recomposite each frame, but blur result cached? With filter and animation, it may re-render blur. Could remove filter and use a gradient only, animate scale. Compositor can handle scale of pre-rasterized layer.

Alternatively animate a small element (like a 20x20 dot) with transform. But visually pointless. Animate the play button sheen (transform) — already exists and is continuous! Wait `.play-sheen` has `animation: playSheen 3.8s ease-in-out infinite`; it should produce frames continuously. Yet RAF log shows only one frame after 162ms. Why no frames? Maybe because the sheen element is inside a button with `overflow: hidden` and transform animation, compositor should produce frames. Hmm, but the page is headless/occluded so compositor throttles animations? Playwright screenshot still captures.

Maybe CSS animations are throttled when page is hidden/occluded (headless). rAF gets throttled too. The measurement rAF forced frames because... actually Chrome does not throttle rAF to 1fps unless page hidden; headless might mark page visible. Hmm.

Let's check `document.visibilityState` and `document.hasFocus()` in headless. Also test adding a setInterval that toggles something, since timers run and trigger frames. `requestAnimationFrame` might be throttled without user gesture? Chrome throttles cross-origin iframes, not main.

Given the observed behavior, perhaps Chrome's "BeginFrame" is paused when no animation frames requested? But `.play-sheen` animation is running. Unless our instrumentation wrapper broke Playwright? No, trace script wrapped rAF but capture isn't wrapped. The wrapper could affect scheduling? It only wraps callbacks. The callback of game loop schedules next frame only if state != menu; menu stops. The CSS animation should still request frames independently, not via JS rAF.

Let's inspect `document.getAnimations()` playState in trace. Also check `visibilityState`. And test `setInterval` vs `requestAnimationFrame` tick. Maybe simpler: keep the game's menu loop running but drastically reduce render frequency and cost: on menu, render one frame every 500ms? This provides frames periodically; stability still needs two frames close together? Playwright's stability check waits until bounding box is same across two consecutive frames. If frames are 500ms apart, it passes in 1s (within 2s). But rendering costs 500ms each → 1s per 2 frames, borderline. Could render a cheap menu variant (just start screen over static game canvas). Actually the start screen covers most of the canvas; we could skip world render entirely on menu after first frame. Then continuous loop is cheap (just update ambient and maybe not render). But rAF callbacks alone may not produce compositor frames? If callback schedules next rAF, it does request a frame each time; Chrome will produce them at vsync. If no visual change, it may skip paint but still count as frames. That's likely enough for Playwright's stable check, which runs its own rAF in the page; the page's continuous rAF keeps the frames flowing.

Our previous "blank raf" test still got menu fps 1.6 with all effects (slow paint). That means rAF did run, but each frame took 600ms. After CSS fix, menu fps 45. So a blank rAF with fixed CSS should give 60. Let's test trial with a blank rAF on current build. Also test whether the game's own continuous menu loop (cheap no-render) is viable.

But wait — why did Playwright not get frames despite CSS animations running? Maybe because animations are on compositor and JS rAF (Playwright's) still needs main-thread frames; headless with no BeginFrame? The measurement rAF loop itself causes frames. So keep a JS rAF loop.

Best fix: don't stop the menu loop entirely. Continue `requestAnimationFrame(this.loop)` while menu, but render only when needed or at low frequency. This keeps JS rAF frames at ~60fps (cheap update) and Playwright actionability becomes fast. However, the first menu render cost is big; subsequent frames can skip `render()`. The loop does `update()` and `render()` currently. We can add:

```
loop(now) {
  ...
  this.update(dt);
  if (this.state !== 'menu') this.render();
  else if (this.menuNeedsRender) { this.render(); this.menuNeedsRender = false; }
  requestAnimationFrame(this.loop);
}
```

But update on menu calls computeView, updateAmbient, updateParticles etc., updateHud, updateTargetChip every frame → some DOM writes every frame (updateHud writes textContent/timers, wind, etc.) which is waste and may cause paints. Add an early cheap path for menu: only update ambient and maybe time; requestAnimationFrame. This keeps frames.

Or simply in loop:
```
if (this.state === 'menu') {
  this.time += dt;
  this.updateAmbient(dt);
  this.renderMenuIfDirty(); // maybe every 2s?
  requestAnimationFrame(this.loop);
  return;
}
```

But do we need to render menu at all? The canvas shows the battlefield behind the start screen; to keep it alive, render occasionally (e.g., every 1.5s) or not at all. We can render once on menu entry and then not. The start screen background (semi-opaque) hides most detail anyway, though the blurred game behind is visible in screenshot. We can render once per second to animate clouds? Clouds are cached in worldBg, so nothing moves except dust motes and terrain. Not worth it. Render once.

However, the menu loop's `updateHud` may write DOM each frame and trigger layout; avoid.

Let's implement menu loop:
```
loop(now) {
  ...
  const dt = ...
  if (this.state === 'menu') {
    this.time += dt;
    this.updateAmbient(dt);
    // keep a cheap 60fps JS frame alive for actionability; repaint the
    // menu backdrop only occasionally
    if (!this.menuDrawn) { this.render(); this.menuDrawn = true; }
    requestAnimationFrame(this.loop);
    return;
  }
  ...
}
```
But `this.render()` on menu draws aim preview? state menu, active null → no. Fine.

`startMatch` calls `startLoop()` which returns if running (it will already be running). Need to handle: running remains true; startMatch calls startLoop, no-op. Good. But `lastTime` continues, dt fine.

Remove the special menu stop. On page hide maybe pause via visibilitychange? Could add but not necessary.

Alternatively, only continue loop while start screen visible; after game over return to menu? There's no return to menu; game over overlay and play again starts. After game over state 'over', loop continues (expensive). Fine.

Let's implement and test. This also makes start screen cloud ambient still alive but not repainted; no issue.

Potential problem: rAF loop at 60fps with updateAmbient mutating cloud positions each frame; render only once means no visual. Fine. It's negligible CPU.

But `running` flag: constructor sets running true and RAF. After startMatch `startLoop()` sees running true and returns. Good.

If game loop is paused when page hidden, actionability during capture isn't affected.

Let's edit loop. Also ensure menu's first render happens (currently loop renders once then stops; new code renders once). Use `this.menuDrawn` reset in setupWorld and set false. Actually setupWorld sets state menu; if called on restart, we want a menu render? startMatch immediately hides start screen, no need. Set `this.menuDrawn = false` in constructor or setup. In loop, if state menu and !menuDrawn, render. Start screen initially shown.

Then when startMatch is called, state becomes aim before next loop, so render normally.

Implement:
```
loop(now) {
 if (!this.running) return;
 ...
 if (this.state === 'menu') {
   this.time += dt;
   this.updateAmbient(dt);
   if (!this.menuDrawn) { this.render(); this.menuDrawn = true; }
   requestAnimationFrame(this.loop);
   return;
 }
 this.update(dt);
 this.render();
 requestAnimationFrame(this.loop);
}
```
Note update() already does time += dt. For menu path, avoid calling update to skip HUD writes. But `render()` uses this.time; fine.

Set `this.menuDrawn = false` in constructor and setupWorld? On startMatch, state changes, so next loop renders. On game over, state over. If we ever return to menu (not currently), reset. In constructor before RAF, menuDrawn false.

Let's edit.

